A||||6||Posthumane||Clutch Pedal (again)||||1||
Z||000000||Posthumane||07-04-2001||05:50 PM||||I know this has been talked about b4, but i can't tell wether my clutch pedal just bent more or if i developed a cylinder problem. I was driving along today, and after a rather quick shift, my clutch pedal developed about 2 inches of travel before it starts to slip. I have to push it all the way to the floor to disengage and after engaging it all the way it comes up about two inches to the rest position. I looked at the pedal before this happened, and it was bent in the rod between the pivot and the pedal. It doesn't really look any more bent than before. Where else on the pedal assy can it bend?||||reg||1
Z||000001||MontanaFiero||07-04-2001||05:58 PM||||check the slave cylinder and the linkage at the transmission for leaks or to see if something has come loose.  Also, there is a known issue concerning clutch pedals and replacing them with improved pedals that won't bend.... do a search.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||07-04-2001||09:25 PM||||Aluminium pedals are the ones prone to bending.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||07-04-2001||09:56 PM||||Which Fieros came with the steel clutch pedals stock?  My '86 GT has almost 200k miles on her and I'm pretty sure the clutch pedal isn't bent.  I haven't tried the magnet test yet.  (Keep forgetting.)<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||BlackARROW||07-05-2001||10:54 PM||||Hi Posthumane,<P>well I guess you have the same clutch problem as I have. Just don't try to bleed and bleed it again - it won't work, and probably it will give you more sweat than anything else.  I'm not ready to fix it, but I seriously believe that it's a faulty bearing; caused by a too-tight Fork bushing or just a misplacement.<P>Here is what I've found on the Fiero Online Service Guide:<P>"You know something is wrong with your clutch when you experience one or more of the following symptoms:<P>1- clutch pedal gets hard to push<BR>2- transmission begins to grind going into reverse<BR>3- odd clutch release points (point where the car begins to move varies, sometimes at the top of the pedal travel, sometimes at the bottom, other times in the middle)<BR>4- clutch fails to engage when cold (you push in the clutch, select a gear, let out the clutch and nothing happens. Several seconds or even minutes later, it engages and you drive off)<BR>5- transmission will not go into gear while engine running. (If you turn off the engine, engage 1st gear, you can then drive off. Gear changes are made on the synchros of the transmission, not the clutch release.)<BR>6- clutch slips (Doesn't drive at all! Even a new clutch may not last 60 seconds while slipping!)<BR>7- release bearing rattles. (Release bearing may normally make a slight noise when pedal depressed but stop when pedal released)<BR>8- sometimes the release bearing presses against the pressure plate at all times. (The bearing is not designed to run constantly. When it fails, it will burn a hole clear thru the pressure plate spring fingers. You will not be able to disengage the clutch if that happens.)"<P>On my case, I have almost all of the symptoms mentionned   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Actually, #1, 2, 3, 5 and 7.<P>Refer to <A HREF="http://thefieroclub.com/osg/fiero_osg/clutch-bushings.html" TARGET=_blank>http://thefieroclub.com/osg/fiero_osg/clutch-bushings.html</A>  for more details.<P>BlackARROW<BR>'85 2m4 <BR><p>[This message has been edited by BlackARROW (edited 07-06-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000005||ROB MAC||07-05-2001||11:00 PM||||I kept trying to bleed mine 2-3 days after it got a bit stiff to push and I snapped the release crossarm thing. $300.00 for the part.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BlackARROW||07-06-2001||09:18 AM||||Here's MM:<P>"1984 thru 1986 originally had aluminum pedals and they have an inherent design flaw."  (reference to Archie's archive #7, <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch7.htm)" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch7.htm)</A> <P>Maybe you got the steel pedal from a late '86 model.  Try the magnet test to get a peace of mind.<P>BlackARROW<BR>'85 2m4 ||||reg||1
